<h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section A: Long Answer Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Answer all questions. Show necessary circuit analysis and steps clearly.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q1.</strong> An electrical circuit is set up with a DC voltage source connected to two resistors in series. Calculate the equivalent resistance of the combination and the current flowing through the circuit.</div><div style='text-align:center;margin:8px 0;'><img src='data:image/png;base64,' style='max-width:320px;max-height:220px;' alt='Diagram'></div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q2.</strong> Two resistors are connected in a parallel network across a power supply. Determine the effective resistance of the parallel combination and the total current drawn from the source.</div><div style='text-align:center;margin:8px 0;'><img src='data:image/png;base64,' style='max-width:320px;max-height:220px;' alt='Diagram'></div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q3.</strong> A complex resistive network is analyzed using a potential divider setup. Find the voltage drop across the second resistor given the input potential and resistance values.</div><div style='text-align:center;margin:8px 0;'><img src='data:image/png;base64,' style='max-width:320px;max-height:220px;' alt='Diagram'></div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q4.</strong> In a bridge circuit arrangement, four resistors are connected. Calculate the current flowing through the galvanometer arm when the bridge is unbalanced with the given parameters.</div><div style='text-align:center;margin:8px 0;'><img src='data:image/png;base64,' style='max-width:320px;max-height:220px;' alt='Diagram'></div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q5.</strong> Three resistors of values R<sub>1</sub> = 4 &Omega;, R<sub>2</sub> = 6 &Omega;, and R<sub>3</sub> = 12 &Omega; are connected in parallel across a 24 V battery. Find the total current in the circuit and the current flowing through each resistor.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q6.</strong> State Joule's law of heating. An electric iron consumes energy at a rate of 840 W when heating is at the maximum rate and 360 W when the heating is at the minimum. The voltage is 220 V. Calculate the current and the resistance in each case.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q7.</strong> Define electric power and write its SI unit. Derive the relation between power, potential difference, and resistance in an electrical circuit. A 100 W bulb is connected to a 220 V supply. Calculate the resistance of the bulb.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q8.</strong> On what factors does the resistance of a conductor depend? Explain each factor with the help of appropriate formulas. A copper wire of length 2 m and area of cross-section 1.7 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup> m<sup>2</sup> has a resistance of 2 &times; 10<sup>-2</sup> &Omega;. Calculate the resistivity of copper.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q9.</strong> Explain why parallel circuit arrangement is preferred over series circuit arrangement for domestic wiring. Give any three valid reasons supporting your answer.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q10.</strong> An electric refrigerator rated 400 W operates 8 hours a day and an electric toaster rated 300 W operates 2 hours a day. What is the cost of the energy to operate them for 30 days at ₹ 3.00 per kWh?</div>
